//
//  Program:    govax, a "Virtual VAX" written in Go
//
//  Module:     DEBUG.HELP
//
//  Purpose:    The documentation for the govax debugger, read by the
//              debugger's HELP command (DBG> HELP). The format is the
//              same as VAX.HELP's, whose preamble explains it: a "$" line
//              holds a topic's key, one four-character word for each
//              word typed after HELP.
//
//  History:    10/05/26    Started, with the commands the debugger has so
//                          far: EXIT, QUIT, HELP, and @ (docs/PHASE-42.md,
//                          subtask 3).
//              10/05/26    Added GO, CALL, and STEP (subtask 4).
//              10/05/26    STEP as VMS's (subtask 7).
//              10/05/26    Every command and qualifier the debugger has
//                          (subtask 15).
//

$HELP
  The govax debugger examines and controls the emulated VAX. It is modeled
  on the VMS debugger, and its commands follow that debugger's syntax. Type
  HELP topic for more about a command, and HELP topic keyword for a
  keyword or a qualifier of it (HELP SET BREAK, HELP EXAMINE /INSTRUCTION).

  Additional information available:

  @           ASM         CALL        CANCEL      CLEAR       DEPOSIT
  EVALUATE    EXAMINE     EXIT        GO          HELP        QUIT
  SET         SHOW        STEP        SYMBOLIZE

  Other topics:

  EXPRESSIONS     How an address or a value is written
  REGISTERS       The names of the registers
  RADIX           The numbers' radix
  BREAKPOINTS     The kinds of eventpoints and what they report

  The debugger is a separate command set from the console's: the VMS
  command line (DIRECTORY, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) isn't available here.
  EXIT returns to the console, where it is.

$EXPR
$EXPRESSIONS
  An address or a value is an expression, which may hold:

    numbers          in the input radix (hexadecimal unless SET RADIX says
                     otherwise); ^X, ^D, ^O, and ^B force a radix, as in
                     MACRO (^D35)
    symbols          a routine, label, or data name from the program's
                     debug symbols, or the console's symbol table
    path names       MODULE\ROUTINE\LABEL, as in DBGDIS\START\LOOP
    lines            %LINE n, or MODULE\%LINE n, the code of a source line
    registers        R0 to R11, AP, FP, SP, PC, PSL (also %R0 and so on),
                     as their values; PC alone is the program counter
    .                the current location: the address EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, or
                     SET BASE last used
    .name            the contents at an address: .PC, .R1+4
    operators        + - * / (and parentheses)

  An address range is start:end (EXAMINE A:B), and a list is separated by
  commas (EXAMINE A,B). A routine's name stands for the address after its
  entry mask when a breakpoint is set on it.

$REGI
$REGISTERS
  The registers the debugger knows:

    R0 to R11, AP (R12), FP (R13), SP (R14), PC (R15)   general registers
    PSL                                                 processor status

  and the privileged registers: P0BR P0LR P1BR P1LR SBR SLR PCBB SCBB IPL
  ASTLVL SIRR SISR ICCS NICR ICR TODR RXCS RXDB TXCS TXDB TBDR SAVISP SAVPC
  SAVPSL WCSA WCSB MAPEN TBIA TBIS PMR SID TBCHK.

  EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, and SHOW show or change them, by name.

$RADI
$RADIX
  The debugger reads numbers, and shows them, in a radix: hexadecimal until
  SET RADIX says otherwise. The input radix is the one numbers typed are
  read in; the output radix is the one values are shown in. SET RADIX sets
  both (or SET RADIX/INPUT, SET RADIX/OUTPUT, one of them), SHOW RADIX
  shows them, and CANCEL RADIX restores hexadecimal. The console has a
  radix of its own (SET RADIX there); the two don't affect each other.

$BREA
$BREAKPOINTS
  The debugger has three kinds of eventpoint:

    breakpoints    stop the program before an instruction runs
                   (SET BREAK), or when a class of instruction is reached
                   (/CALL, /BRANCH, /RETURN, /LINE, /INSTRUCTION), or when
                   an exception is signaled (/EXCEPTION), or, govax's own,
                   when a machine fault is about to be delivered (/FAULT)
    tracepoints    report where the program is and go on (SET TRACE)
    watchpoints    stop after the instruction that changes a location
                   (SET WATCH)

  The report says where, in the names the program's debug symbols give:
  "break at DBGDIS\START\%LINE 42" or "trace on lines at ...". SHOW BREAK,
  SHOW TRACE, and SHOW WATCH list them, and CANCEL takes them away.

$@
$INCL
  Runs the debugger commands in a command file.

  Format:

    @file-spec

  The file is read a line at a time, as typed at the DBG> prompt. If a
  command in it ends the debugger session (EXIT), the lines after it are
  console commands.

$CALL
  Calls a routine under the debugger.

  Format:

    CALL[/STEP] routine [(argument[,argument...])]

  The arguments are pushed as a CALLS instruction pushes them. The routine
  runs until it returns, a breakpoint stops it, or Ctrl-C interrupts it.

  /STEP runs only the routine's first instruction and stops. (/BREAK,
  /DEBUG, and /DBG mean the same.)

$CANC
$CLEA
  Removes eventpoints and settings: the debugger's CANCEL. CLEAR is a
  synonym for it.

  Format:

    CANCEL keyword [qualifiers] [parameter]

  Additional information available:

  BREAK  INTERRUPT  MEMORY  MODE  RADIX  SOURCE  SYMBOL  TB  TRACE  WATCH

$CANC,BREA
$CLEA,BREA
  Removes breakpoints.

  Format:

    CANCEL BREAK [qualifier] [address[,address...]]

  With addresses, removes the breakpoints there. With a qualifier, removes
  the breakpoints of that kind:

    /ALL            every breakpoint
    /CALL           the breakpoints on calls
    /BRANCH         the breakpoints on branches
    /LINE           the breakpoints on source lines
    /RETURN         the breakpoints on returns
    /EXCEPTION      the breakpoint on exceptions
    /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)]
                    the instruction breakpoints (of the opcodes named, or
                    all of them)
    /FAULT[=code]   govax's fault breakpoints (the one for the fault code,
                    or all of them)

  Cancelling when none is set is %DEBUG-I-NOBREAKS.

$CANC,TRAC
$CLEA,TRAC
  Removes tracepoints.

  Format:

    CANCEL TRACE [qualifier] [address[,address...]]

  The qualifiers are /ALL, /CALL, /BRANCH, /LINE, /RETURN, and
  /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)], as CANCEL BREAK has them.

$CANC,WATC
$CLEA,WATC
  Removes watchpoints.

  Format:

    CANCEL WATCH [/ALL] [location[,location...]]

$CANC,SOUR
$CLEA,SOUR
  Forgets the directories SET SOURCE named, so source files are looked for
  where the image says they are.

  Format:

    CANCEL SOURCE

$CANC,RADI
$CLEA,RADI
  Restores the radix to hexadecimal, for input and output.

  Format:

    CANCEL RADIX

$CANC,MODE
$CLEA,MODE
  Restores SET MODE's defaults (symbolic names, operands explained, and the
  rest).

  Format:

    CANCEL MODE

$CANC,INTE
$CLEA,INTE
  Removes a queued interrupt (govax's own).

  Format:

    CANCEL INTERRUPT [/ALL] [vector]

  vector is the interrupt's SCB offset, in hexadecimal. /ALL removes every
  queued interrupt.

$CANC,TB
$CLEA,TB
  Empties the translation buffer (govax's own), the cache of recent virtual
  to physical address translations. The next access to each page walks the
  page tables again.

  Format:

    CANCEL TB

$CANC,MEMO
$CLEA,MEMO
  CANCEL MEMORY/STATISTICS zeroes the memory system's counters (govax's
  own). (At the console, CLEAR MEMORY with no qualifier is a different
  command, which zeroes the memory itself.)

  Format:

    CANCEL MEMORY/STATISTICS

$CANC,SYMB
$CLEA,SYMB
  Removes symbols from the machine's symbol table (govax's own): the table
  ASM and SET SYMBOL define symbols in, and SHOW SYMBOL/ALL lists. System
  symbols are never removed. (At the console, DELETE/SYMBOL deletes DCL
  symbols, which are a different table.)

  Format:

    CANCEL SYMBOL name
    CANCEL SYMBOL/ALL
    CANCEL SYMBOL/TEMPORARY

  /ALL removes every user symbol, and /TEMPORARY every user symbol not
  defined with SET/PERMANENT.

$DEPO
$D
  Stores a value at a location: a register, or an address.

  Format:

    DEPOSIT[/type] location = value
    DEPOSIT/ASCII[:count] location = "string"

  The value is in the input radix. The type is /BYTE, /WORD, /LONGWORD, or
  /QUADWORD; without one it is the type of the data the location names in
  the program's debug symbols, else a longword. /ASCII stores a quoted
  string, of the count given (padded with blanks) or its own length.

  Examples:

    DEPOSIT R0 = 5
    DEPOSIT PC = 200
    DEPOSIT/BYTE 2000 = 0FF
    DEPOSIT COUNT = 10

$EVAL
  Shows the value of an expression.

  Format:

    EVALUATE[/radix] expression
    EVALUATE/ADDRESS expression

  The value is shown in the output radix, or in /HEXADECIMAL, /DECIMAL,
  /OCTAL, or /BINARY. With a data name, the value is its contents.
  /ADDRESS shows the address instead.

$EXAM
$EX
  Shows what is at a location.

  Format:

    EXAMINE[/qualifier...] [location[,location...]]
    EXAMINE[/qualifier...] start:end

  A location is an expression (HELP EXPRESSIONS): an address, a name, a
  register. A range (start:end) shows each item from start through end. With
  no location, EXAMINE shows the next location after the last one shown.

  Without /INSTRUCTION, EXAMINE shows data: typed by the program's debug
  symbols where the location names a datum (arrays as TABLE[0:3], strings
  through their descriptors), and otherwise by the type qualifier given, in
  the output radix.

  Additional information available:

  /INSTRUCTION  /OPERANDS  /BYTE  /WORD  /LONGWORD  /QUADWORD  /ASCII
  /HEXADECIMAL  /DECIMAL  /OCTAL  /BINARY  /PSL  /PTE  /CONSTANTS
  /SHAREABLE  /SYMBOLIC

$EXAM,/INS
  Shows the machine instruction at each location, in the VMS debugger's
  layout, with names from the program's debug symbols:

    DBGDIS\START\%LINE 42:  MOVL     S^#0A,R2

  A range lists every instruction in it.

$EXAM,/OPE
  With /INSTRUCTION, explains each operand: its value, and where it is.
  /OPERANDS=FULL says more. /NOOPERANDS (the default, unless SET MODE
  OPERANDS) leaves them out.

$EXAM,/BYT
$EXAM,/WOR
$EXAM,/LON
$EXAM,/QUA
  Show each item as a byte, a word (two bytes), a longword (four), or a
  quadword (eight), when the program's debug symbols don't give the type.

$EXAM,/ASC
  Shows bytes as ASCII text. /ASCII:count shows count bytes.

$EXAM,/HEX
$EXAM,/DEC
$EXAM,/OCT
$EXAM,/BIN
  Show the value in hexadecimal, decimal, octal, or binary, for this
  command, instead of the output radix.

$EXAM,/PSL
  govax's own: shows a longword as a processor status longword, field by
  field.

$EXAM,/PTE
  govax's own: shows a longword as a page table entry: its valid bit,
  protection, modify bit, owner, and page frame number.

$EXAM,/CON
  govax's own, with /INSTRUCTION: names a constant by the symbol that
  defines it (MOVL S^#DBGDIS\LIMIT,R2), which the VMS debugger doesn't.

$EXAM,/SHA
  govax's own, with /INSTRUCTION: names a G^ reference's routine in a
  shareable image (CALLS S^#01,G^LIB$PUT_OUTPUT).

$EXAM,/SYM
  Accepted, as VMS has it. SET MODE SYMBOLIC or NOSYMBOLIC chooses whether
  an instruction's addresses are names.

$EXIT
  Ends the debugger session and returns to the console. The machine is
  left as it is.

  Format:

    EXIT

$GO
  Runs the program, from the current PC or from an address.

  Format:

    GO [address]

  The program runs until it halts, a breakpoint stops it, or Ctrl-C
  interrupts it. A breakpoint at the address the run starts from doesn't
  stop it at once.

$HELP,HELP
  Displays help on a debugger command.

  Format:

    HELP [topic ...]

  HELP alone lists the topics.

$QUIT
  Ends the debugger session and returns to the console, as EXIT does.
  (On VMS, QUIT skips the program's exit handlers, which EXIT runs.
  govax's debugger doesn't run exit handlers yet, so the two are the
  same.)

  Format:

    QUIT

$SET
$SE
  Changes what the debugger does or shows, or sets machine state.

  Format:

    SET keyword [qualifiers] [parameters]

  Additional information available:

  BASE     BREAK    FAULT    MODE     MODULE   PSL      PTE   
  QUANTUM  RADIX    SOURCE   STEP     TRACE    VM       WATCH

  (SET BREAKPOINT, SET TRACEPOINT, and SET WATCHPOINT are the same as
  SET BREAK, SET TRACE, and SET WATCH.)

$SET,BREA
$SE,BREA
  Sets breakpoints: the program stops before the instruction runs.

  Format:

    SET BREAK [qualifiers] [address[,address...]] [WHEN (condition)]
              [DO (command[;command...])]

  An address may be a routine's name: the break is after its entry mask, at
  its first instruction. A qualifier picks a class instead of an address;
  one at most is given:

    /CALL           each call instruction (CALLS, JSB, ...)
    /BRANCH         each branch instruction
    /RETURN routine each RET that ends a call of the routine named
    /LINE           each source line's first instruction
    /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)]
                    each instruction (of the opcodes named, if any)
    /EXCEPTION      each exception signaled
    /FAULT=code     govax's own: when the machine fault with that code
                    (hexadecimal) is about to be delivered

  /AFTER:n skips the first n times the breakpoint is reached. /TEMPORARY
  removes it when it first stops the program. WHEN (condition) stops only
  when the condition is nonzero; DO runs the commands at the stop.

  The report is "break at location", "break on lines at ...", and so on.
  A break at the address a run starts from doesn't stop it at once.

  Examples:

    SET BREAK FACT
    SET BREAK/TEMPORARY DBGDIS\START\%LINE 47
    SET BREAK/INSTRUCTION=(MOVC3)
    SET BREAK SUB2 WHEN (R0 EQL 5) DO (EXAMINE R0)

$SET,TRAC
$SE,TRAC
  Sets tracepoints: the program reports where it is and goes on.

  Format:

    SET TRACE [qualifiers] [address[,address...]] [WHEN (condition)]
              [DO (command[;command...])]

  The qualifiers are /AFTER:n, /TEMPORARY, /CALL, /BRANCH, /LINE, /RETURN,
  and /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)], as SET BREAK has them. SET TRACE
  /INSTRUCTION traces every instruction. The report is "trace at
  location", "trace on lines at ...", and so on.

$SET,WATC
$SE,WATC
  Sets watchpoints: the program stops after an instruction that changes a
  location.

  Format:

    SET WATCH [/AFTER:n] [/TEMPORARY] location [WHEN (condition)]
              [DO (command[;command...])]

  The location's size comes from the program's debug symbols: a scalar by
  its type, an array (BUFFER[0:15]) item by item, anything else a
  longword. The report gives the old and new values:

    watch of DBGCMD\COUNT at DBGCMD\START\%LINE 42
       old value: 00000000
       new value: 00000001

$SET,STEP
$SE,STEP
  Sets what STEP does when it isn't told.

  Format:

    SET STEP keyword[,keyword...]

  The keywords are:

    LINE | INSTRUCTION     how far a step goes
    OVER | INTO (IN) | RETURN
                           what to do at a call (RETURN is govax's: run to
                           the return of the routine)
    SILENT | NOSILENT      whether the stop is reported
    SOURCE | NOSOURCE      whether the source line is shown

  SHOW STEP shows the settings.

$SET,SOUR
$SE,SOUR
  Names the directories searched for a program's source files, when they
  aren't where the image says.

  Format:

    SET SOURCE directory[,directory...]

  SHOW SOURCE lists them, and CANCEL SOURCE forgets them.

$SET,MODE
$SE,MODE
  Sets display modes, and govax's access mode.

  Format:

    SET MODE keyword[,keyword...]

  Display modes (the VMS debugger's):

    SYMBOLIC | NOSYMBOLIC  whether an address is shown as a name
    OPERANDS[=FULL|BRIEF] | NOOPERANDS
                           whether EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION explains operands
    LINE | NOLINE          whether the debugger works by source line
    G_FLOAT | NOG_FLOAT    the floating format
    SCROLL, DYNAMIC, INTERRUPT, and their NO forms are accepted; screen
    mode isn't available.

  Access modes (govax's own): KERNEL, EXECUTIVE, SUPERVISOR, USER switch
  the CPU's mode and its stack pointer, and ISP the interrupt stack.

  SHOW MODE shows the settings, and CANCEL MODE restores the defaults.

$SET,RADI
$SE,RADI
  Sets the radix for numbers typed and shown.

  Format:

    SET RADIX [/INPUT|/OUTPUT] radix

  radix is DECIMAL, HEXADECIMAL, OCTAL, or BINARY (also 10, 16, 8, 2).
  With no qualifier it sets both. HELP RADIX says more.

$SET,MODU
$SE,MODU
  Notes that a module's symbols are wanted.

  Format:

    SET MODULE module[,module...]
    SET MODULE/ALL

  govax reads every module's symbols when it loads an image, so this
  changes only the "set" column of SHOW MODULE and which modules a SHOW
  SYMBOL with no IN looks in.

$SET,PROC
$SE,PROC
  Makes a process the visible one: the one whose registers and memory
  EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, SHOW REGISTERS, and the rest show.

  Format:

    SET PROCESS [/VISIBLE] [pid | name]

  pid is a process ID in hexadecimal (302 or 00000302); name is a process
  name, as SHOW PROCESS lists it. With neither, the console's own process
  (process 1) is made visible again. The CPU moves to the process, as
  when a run stops in it. Running is unchanged: STEP and breakpoints
  belong to process 1, and GO, STEP, and CALL give the CPU back to
  process 1 first. A process that isn't there is %SYSTEM-W-NONEXPR.

$SET,PSL
$SE,PSL
  Changes fields of the processor status longword (govax's own).

  Format:

    SET PSL field=value[,field=value...]

  The fields are C, V, Z, N, T, IV, FU, DV, IPL, and MOD (also CUR_MOD).
  Values are in the input radix.

  Example:

    SET PSL IPL=1F, N=1

$SET,PTE
$SET,PAGE
$SE,PTE
$SE,PAGE
  Changes page table entries (govax's own).

  Format:

    SET PTE address [TO address] field=value[,field=value...]

  The fields are VALID (V), PROT, MODIFY (M), OWNER (OWN), SOFT (S), and PFN
  (PAGE), the page frame number. PROT is a number or the name SHOW PTE prints (PTE$K_KW). With
  TO, every 512-byte page from the first address through the second is
  changed.

  Example:

    SET PTE 200 TO 400 VALID=1,PFN=10

$SET,FAUL
$SET,HIST
$SE,FAUL
$SE,HIST
  Sets how many machine faults are remembered for SHOW EXCEPTIONS (govax's
  own).

  Format:

    SET FAULT count

$SET,VM
$SET,MAPE
$SE,VM
$SE,MAPE
  SET VM turns address translation (memory mapping) on, and SET NOVM turns
  it off (govax's own). The privileged register MAPEN is the switch.

  Format:

    SET VM
    SET NOVM

$SET,BASE
$SE,BASE
  Sets the current location, where EXAMINE with no location starts (govax's
  own).

  Format:

    SET BASE address

$SHOW
$SH
  Displays what the debugger knows.

  Format:

    SHOW keyword [qualifiers] [parameters]

  Additional information available:

  BASE       BREAK      CALLS      CLOCK      CPU_STATUS EXCEPTIONS
  IMAGE      INTRUCTION LANGUAGE   MAPS       MEMORY     MODE
  MODULE     PAGE       PSL        QUANTUM    RADIX      REGIONS
  REGISTERS  SCB        SCOPE      SHIM       SOURCE     STACK  
  STEP       SYMBOL     TB         TRACE      WATCH

  and the register names: SHOW R0, SHOW PC, SHOW P0BR, and so on. SHOW SP,
  KSP, ESP, SSP, USP, and ISP dump the longwords on a stack.


$SHOW,QUAN
$SH  ,QUAN

    The SHOW QUANTUM command displays the execution quantum the emulator
    guarantees before allowing VAX interrupts to be processed. This is
    expressed as a number of instructions; after that many instructions
    have been executed, the processes any queued interrupts.  A quantum
    of zero disables the quantum mechanism, and uses the realtime clock
    instead to signal when to handle interrupts. The use of quantum is
    largely to ensure that interrupt behavior is deterministic.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW QUANTUM

$SET ,QUAN

    The SET QUANTUM command sets the execution quantum the emulator
    guarantees before allowing VAX interrupts to be processed. This is
    expressed as a number of instructions; after that many instructions
    have been executed, the processes any queued interrupts.  A quantum
    of zero disables the quantum mechanism, and uses the realtime clock
    instead to signal when to handle interrupts. The use of quantum is
    largely to ensure that interrupt behavior is deterministic.
   
    Syntax:
    
        SET QUANTUM n
    
    Parameter:
    
        n       The number of instructions to be executed before the 
                emulator will process interrupts deterministically.
$SHOW,INST
$SH  ,INST
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION command prints a reminder of the instruction
    opcode values, for every VAX instruction (govax implements them
    all).  The SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/ALL command
    displays additional information about each instructions operands, if
    any.

    Syntax:

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/ALL

$SHOW,INST,/UNI
$SHOW,UNIM,INST
$SH  ,INST,/UNI
$SH  ,UNIM,INST
    The SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/UNIMPLEMENTED command displays a table of the
    VAX instructions govax doesn't implement, whose use would be a
    reserved instruction fault.  govax implements every instruction, so
    the table is empty ("0 Instructions.").

    Syntax: 

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/UNIMPLEMENTED

$SHOW,INST,/PRO
$SH  ,INST,/PRO
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE command displays the number of times
    each VAX instruction has been executed.  This can be used to determine
    where a program is spending it's time, based on the cost of each type
    of instruction.

    You can reset the counters to zero with the CLEAR PROFILE command.

    Syntax:

         SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE

$SHOW,INST,/MOD
$SH  ,INST,/MOD
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE command displays the number of times each VAX
    instruction mode has been executed.  This can be used to determine
    what addressing modes are used, etc..

    You can reset the counters to zero with the CLEAR PROFILE command.

    Syntax:

         SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE

$SHOW,BREA
$SH,BREA
  Lists the breakpoints, with their counts and clauses.

  Format:

    SHOW BREAK

$SHOW,TRAC
$SH,TRAC
  Lists the tracepoints.

  Format:

    SHOW TRACE

$SHOW,WATC
$SH,WATC
  Lists the watchpoints.

  Format:

    SHOW WATCH

$SHOW,STEP
$SH,STEP
  Shows what STEP does when it isn't told (SET STEP).

  Format:

    SHOW STEP

$SHOW,SOUR
$SH,SOUR
  Lists the directories SET SOURCE named.

  Format:

    SHOW SOURCE

$SHOW,CALL
$SH,CALL
  Lists the call frames, innermost first, one row each: module, routine,
  line, and the PC relative to the routine and absolute. A call frame is the
  record CALLS and CALLG leave on the stack, which links each routine to its
  caller.

  Format:

    SHOW CALLS [count]

  With a count, only that many frames. In a JSB subroutine, which has no
  frame of its own, the row is the routine that holds it.

$SHOW,STAC
$SH,STAC
  Describes each call frame in full: the saved registers, argument count, and
  condition handler (VMS's SHOW STACK).

  Format:

    SHOW STACK [count]

  govax's longword dump of a stack is SHOW SP, KSP, ESP, SSP, USP, or ISP.

$SHOW,SP
$SHOW,KSP
$SHOW,ESP
$SHOW,SSP
$SHOW,USP
$SHOW,ISP
$SH,SP
$SH,KSP
$SH,ESP
$SH,SSP
$SH,USP
$SH,ISP
  Dumps the longwords on a stack (govax's own): SHOW SP the current one, and
  the others the stack of the mode named (kernel, executive, supervisor,
  user, and the interrupt stack).

  Format:

    SHOW KSP [count] [/ALL]

  count is how many longwords, in hexadecimal; /ALL shows the whole stack.

$SHOW,REGI
$SHOW,REG
$SH,REGI
$SH,REG
  SHOW REGISTERS shows the general registers and the PSL (govax's own).
  A single register, general or privileged, is SHOW name (SHOW R3, SHOW PC,
  SHOW P0BR). HELP REGISTERS lists the names.

  SHOW REGIONS shows the limits of the P0, P1, and S0 regions image
  activation uses (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW REGISTERS
    SHOW REGIONS

$SHOW,PSL
$SH,PSL
  Shows the processor status longword as a number and as its fields
  (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW PSL

$SHOW,CPU_
$SH,CPU_
  Shows the registers and the PSL, the stack pointer of each mode, and the
  privileged registers, then the current location (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW CPU_STATUS

$SHOW,CLOC
$SH,CLOC
  Shows the interval clock and the time-of-day register (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW CLOCK

$SHOW,BASE
$SH,BASE
  Shows the current location, where EXAMINE with no location starts
  (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW BASE

$SHOW,MEMO
$SHOW,VM
$SH,MEMO
$SH,VM
  Shows the size of physical memory and, once VMINIT has made page tables,
  how much of each region (P0, P1, S0) is used (govax's own). With
  /STATISTICS, counters of the memory system's work; /RUNTIME and /FULL
  show more.

  Format:

    SHOW MEMORY [/STATISTICS] [/RUNTIME] [/FULL]

$SHOW,MAPS
$SH,MAPS
  Reports that govax has no field-offset map of RMS's data structures to
  show (the VMS command of this name shows one; govax's RMS code keeps no
  such table).

  Format:

    SHOW MAPS

$SHOW,TB
$SH,TB
  Shows the translation buffer: its hit and miss counts, and each entry in
  use (govax's own). The translation buffer is the cache of recent virtual
  to physical address translations. Flushes counts the times the whole
  buffer was emptied (MTPR to TBIA); PFlushes the times only its process
  (P0 and P1) translations were, as LDPCTX does when it switches processes.

  Format:

    SHOW TB

$SHOW,PAGE
$SHOW,PTE
$SH,PAGE
$SH,PTE
  Shows the page table entry for an address (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW PAGE [/READ|/WRITE] address

  The entry's fields are shown, with its protection by name (PTE$K_KW).
  /READ and /WRITE say which access the entry is checked for.

$SHOW,SCB
$SH,SCB
  Shows the system control block (SCB), which holds the address of each
  exception and interrupt routine (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW SCB [/ALL]

  Only the vectors in use, unless /ALL.

$SHOW,SHIM
$SH,SHIM
  Shows the shims: the stubs that stand in for shareable images' routines,
  and what each reaches, native code or a microkernel routine (govax's
  own).

  Format:

    SHOW SHIM

$SHOW,EXCE
$SHOW,FAUL
$SH,EXCE
$SH,FAUL
  Shows the machine faults and exceptions recently taken, and the interrupts
  pending (govax's own). SET FAULT chooses how many faults are kept.

  Format:

    SHOW EXCEPTIONS

$SHOW,MODE
$SH,MODE
  Shows the display modes (SET MODE), and, on a last line, govax's access
  mode.

  Format:

    SHOW MODE

$SHOW,RADI
$SH,RADI
  Shows the input and output radix.

  Format:

    SHOW RADIX

$SHOW,IMAG
$SH,IMAG
  Lists the loaded images, the main one marked with an asterisk: whether
  it has debug symbols (set), and its first and last address.

  Format:

    SHOW IMAGE [/FULL]

  /FULL (govax's own) adds each image's counts of modules and routines.

$SHOW,MODU
$SH,MODU
  Lists the modules of the program's debug symbols, whether each is set,
  and the size of its symbol records.

  Format:

    SHOW MODULE

$SHOW,PROC
$SH,PROC
  Lists the processes: each one's PID, name, scheduling state (as SHOW
  SYSTEM shows it), and PC, the visible one (see SET PROCESS) marked
  with "*".

  Format:

    SHOW PROCESS

$SHOW,SYMB
$SH,SYMB
  Lists the symbols matching a pattern.

  Format:

    SHOW SYMBOL [/ADDRESS] [/TYPE] pattern [IN module[,module...]]

  The pattern may have * and % wildcards, and is matched with the symbol's
  name, or its whole path name if it has a backslash. The symbols are the
  routines first, then the data and labels, then the program sections, each
  by name. /ADDRESS shows each one's address, and /TYPE its data type.

  A pattern that matches no symbol of the program is tried against the
  machine's symbol table (the symbols ASM defines, and the predefined
  system symbols such as PTE$K_UR and VAX$PR_SBR), and then is
  %DEBUG-E-NOSYMBOL.

  SHOW SYMBOL/ALL and SHOW SYMBOL/SYSTEM (govax's own) list the machine's
  symbol table alone: every symbol, or only the system symbols. The pattern
  is then optional.

    SHOW SYMBOL/ALL [pattern]
    SHOW SYMBOL/SYSTEM [pattern]

  For example, SHOW SYMBOL/SYSTEM CONSOLE$*. (At the console, SHOW SYMBOL
  shows DCL symbols, a different table.)

$SHOW,SCOP
$SH,SCOP
  Lists the call levels names are looked up in, innermost (level 0, marked
  with an asterisk) first. A routine entered more than once is numbered
  (FACT 1).

  Format:

    SHOW SCOPE

$SHOW,LANG
$SH,LANG
  Shows the language of the current module (MACRO, for the programs govax
  runs).

  Format:

    SHOW LANGUAGE

$STEP
$ST
$S
  Executes one or more steps of the program and stops again. A step is
  one source line, or one instruction, as SET STEP says.

  Format:

    STEP[/qualifier...] [count]

  count is the number of steps to take (one if none is given); only where
  the last one ends is reported.

  /LINE (the default) steps to the first instruction of the next source
  line. /INSTRUCTION steps one instruction. Where the program has no line
  numbers, a step is one instruction either way.

  /OVER (the default) runs a call (CALLS, CALLG, JSB, BSBB, BSBW, or a
  change-mode instruction) to its end, and the step stops after it. /INTO
  (also /IN) follows the call into the routine.

  /RETURN runs until the RET that ends the routine's call frame is about
  to execute. The wait belongs to that frame: a deeper call's RET doesn't
  end it, and it stays in force across breakpoints and other STEPs until
  the RET is reached.

  /BRANCH and /CALL run until the next branch or call instruction is about
  to execute.

  /SILENT reports nothing. /NOSILENT (the default) reports where the
  step stopped. /[NO]SOURCE chooses whether the source line is shown with
  it.

  With a qualifier left out, STEP uses what SET STEP chose.

$SYMB
  Shows each name the debugger has for an address.

  Format:

    SYMBOLIZE address

  The program's routines, labels, and lines come first, then the names in
  the console's symbol table. An address with no name is shown as itself.

  Example:

    SYMBOLIZE 5A0

$ASM ,PSEU,SHIM
$ASSE,PSEU,SHIM
    The .SHIM pseudo-operand creates a software shim entry.  This is used in
    the micro-kernel to map runtime library vector points to support routines
    in the micro-kernel.  Some of these support routines are implemented in
    the micro-kernel itself, and some transfer control to native runtime code
    in the govax emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        .SHIM   <entry-name>, [<runtime-code>], <runtime-library>, <offset>
    
    Parameters:
    
        <entry-name>        This is the name of the entry point that supports
                            the runtime function.  It may be the name of an
                            already-defined entry point in the micro-kernel,
                            or a shim stub will be generated by default with
                            this name.
        
        <runtime-code>      If specified, this is the dispatch code for the
                            integrated runtime library in the native emulator
                            code.  See the files shim.c and rtl.c for more
                            information.  If omitted or zero, then a shim
                            stub is not create, and entry-name is assumed to
                            specify a real VAX support routine in the micro-
                            kernel.
        
        <runtime-library>   The name of the runtime library that this is an
                            entry point shim for, such as LIBRTL or DECC$SHR.
                            Do not include the ".EXE" suffix.  This name must
                            match the name of a sharable library list entry
                            in an image for the shim to be bound to the image.
        
        <offset>            This is the offset into the sharable image library
                            where the routine resides.  Usually this is an
                            offset near the start of the actual sharable image
                            code that is a jump vector to the "real" routine.
                            In any case, this offset value (a longword) must
                            exactly match the offset in the image to be bound.
    
    Example:
    
        .SHIM   LIB$PUT_OUTPUT, 0, LIBRTL, ^X0478
        
        This indicates that when an image is loaded that includes a sharable
        image reference to LIBRTL+^X0478, then those references will be remapped
        to the LIB$PUT_OUTPUT routine.  Because the runtime library dispatch 
        code is zero, the routine must be a VAX routine, already assembled in 
        memory.
        
        
        .SHIM   STR$UPCASE, 2, LIBRTL, ^X0778
        
        This indicates that when an image is loaded with a reference to 
        LIBRTL+^X0778, it will call a shim stub named STR$UPCASE.  The shim stub 
        is created as part of the assembly of the .SHIM instruction.  This stub
        will use the special XFC instruction to transfer control to the RTL 
        support routine for code 2.  See shim.c and rtl.c for more information.
        
$ASM 
$ASSE
    The ASM command invokes the pseudo assembler.  This can be used to store
    instructions or data in memory.  It recognizes the standard VAX assembler
    syntax.  It can create a symbol table, including forward references.  It
    can also be used to set the attributes of the processor itself.

    The assembly continues until the end of the input file is read or a
    .END pseudo opcode is found.  See the help on ASM PSEUDO for more
    information on supported pseudo-opcodes, ASM LABELS for labels,
    local labels, and direct assignment, ASM ADDRESSING for how
    operands are encoded, and ASM EXPRESSIONS for expressions.

    A statement whose last character before any comment is a hyphen
    ("-") continues on the next line.
    
    Syntax:

        ASM  [<file>]

    Parameter:

        <file>       You can optionally specify a file on disk that is to
                     be read in and assembled.  If you do not specify a 
                     file then the console prompt changes to ASM> and the
                     assembler statements are entered from the keyboard.

$ASM ,LABE

    A statement may start with a label, which defines a symbol whose
    value is the current location:

        NAME:       An ordinary label.
        NAME::      A label that survives .CLEAR.
        10$:        A local label: decimal digits followed by "$".

    A local label is only known within its local label block, the code
    between two ordinary labels (.ENTRY, .SCOPE, .SHIM, .REGION, and
    .END also end a block).  The same local label, such as 10$, can be
    used again in each block.  Referring to a local label that isn't
    defined in the block before the block ends is an error.  Local
    labels are not kept in the console's symbol table.

    A symbol can also be given a value with a direct assignment:

        NAME = <expression>
        NAME == <expression>
        . = <expression>        Sets the current location.

    The expression may not contain forward references.

$ASM ,ADDR

    Operands use the VAX MACRO addressing mode syntax:

        Rn                  Register
        (Rn)                Register deferred
        (Rn)+  -(Rn)        Autoincrement, autodecrement
        @(Rn)+              Autoincrement deferred
        #value              Short literal, or immediate if it won't fit
        S^#value  I^#value  Short literal, immediate
        disp(Rn)  @disp(Rn) Displacement [deferred]
        B^ W^ L^disp(Rn)    Displacement of a given size
        address   @address  Relative [deferred]: PC plus a displacement
        B^ W^ L^address     Relative, with a displacement of a given size
        @#address           Absolute
        operand[Rx]         Indexed

    With no B^, W^, or L^ prefix, a displacement or relative address
    whose value is already known uses the smallest displacement that
    holds it.  A forward reference uses a longword displacement.  A
    relative operand is stored as the distance from the end of the
    operand to the address, so code that uses it must run at the
    address it was assembled for, or be moved as a whole.

$ASM ,EXPR

    Expressions follow VAX MACRO.  A number is decimal unless a radix
    operator says otherwise, and all binary operators have the same
    priority, applied left to right: 1+2*3 is 9, and 1+<2*3> is 7.

        <expression>        Grouping; ( ) also works
        + - * /             Arithmetic
        A@B                 Shift A left B bits (right if B is negative)
        A&B  A!B  A\B       Logical AND, inclusive OR, exclusive OR
        -A  +A              Negation
        ^C A                One's complement
        ^D ^X ^O ^B         Decimal, hexadecimal, octal, binary: ^X1F,
                            or ^X<10+20> for a whole expression
        ^A/text/            ASCII: up to four characters, any delimiter
        'text'              ASCII, as above
        ^M<R2,R3>           Register mask
        .                   The current location

    A number must be all digits of its radix: 0FF is an error, where
    ^X0FF is 255.  The console's SET RADIX does not affect the
    assembler, and disassembly shows hexadecimal values with ^X so
    that it can be assembled again.

    An expression may use a symbol that is defined later in the program
    (a forward reference) where it adds or subtracts that symbol, or
    multiplies it by a constant: SYM+8, B-A, 2*SYM, SYM-.  The value is
    completed when the symbol is defined.  Anything else done to a
    forward reference is an error.

$ASM ,PSEU

    The assembler supports a number of "pseudo-opcodes" which are really
    instructions to the assembler rather than actual VAX opcodes.  You can
    get help on each of the pseudo-opcodes.  To get help, use the command
    HELP ASM PSEUDO followed by the name of the pseudo-opcode.

    Note that in the actual assembler code, pseudo opcodes are identified
    by a leading "." character.  Do not specify this in the help command.

        ALIGN
        ASCIC
        ASCID
        ASCII
        ASCIZ
        BASE
        BLKB
        BLKD
        BLKF
        BLKL
        BLKW
        BYTE
        CASE
        CLEAR
        CONSOLE
        D_FLOAT
        END
        ENDM
        ENDR
        ENTRY
        ERROR
        F_FLOAT
        G_FLOATING
        H_FLOATING
        IF
        IIF
        INCLUDE
        IRP
        IRPC
        LONG
        MACRO
        MDELETE
        MEXIT
        MODE
        NARG
        NCHR
        NTYPE
        OCTA
        PACKED
        PRINT
        PTE
        QUAD
        REGION
        REPEAT
        SCB
        SET
        SHIM
        WARN
        WORD
        VECTOR
        
$ASM ,PSEU,VECT

    The VECTOR directive starts assembling code that is stored in the
    SCB vector.  The address of the code following the directive is
    stored in the SCB slot.

    Syntax:

        .VECTOR  <slot-number> [, <stack-name> ]

    Parameter:

        <slot-number>   This is the number of the slot that will be
                        filled with the address of the following
                        instructions.  The address is aligned on an
                        8-byte boundary automatically.  The slot
                        number is usually expressed symbolically,
                        such as with EXC$ARITH for the arithmentic
                        trap slot.

        <stack-name>    This can be either ISP or KSP.  If omitted,
                        then KSP is assumed.  This indicates if the
                        fault runs on the interrupt stack (with VM
                        turned off) or not.

$ASM ,PSEU,CLEA

    The CLEAR directive will clear all symbols from the user symbol table,
    or a specific symbol.  If no name is given then all symbols will be
    cleared.

    Syntax:

        .CLEAR   [ <symbol-name> ]

$ASM ,PSEU,SET 

    The SET directive sets a symbol in the symbol table.  This can often
    be used in conjunction with expressions containing the "." operator
    which means the current location being assembled into.

    Syntax:

        .SET   <symbol-name> <value>

    Parameters:

        <symbol-name>     The name of the symbol to create or set.

        <value>           Any valid expression.

$ASM ,PSEU,SCB 

    The SCB directive allows you to define a system control block address
    for catching system exceptions.  These are stored in the SCB by the
    assembler, using the current value of the SCBB base register.

    Syntax:

        .SCB  <exception>, <address> [, <stack>]


    Parameters:

        <exception>     This is the exception code.  This must be a
                        multiple of 4.  Usually, it is defined using
                        one of the predefined symbols.  For example,
                        use the symbol EXC$ACCVIO to indicate the
                        Access Violation exception handler slot in 
                        the SCB.

        <address>       This is the address of the exception handler.
                        It must be a quadword aligned address.

        <stack>         If given, this may be ISP or KSP to indicate
                        which stack the handler is to run on.  If
                        omitted, then the Kernel Stack Pointer is
                        assumed.

$ASM ,PSEU,ALIG

    The ALIGN directive advances the current location if needed
    to ensure that it is an even multiple of the alignment value.
    For example, a system exception handler must start on a quad-
    word boundary, so an .ALIGN 8 can be used to ensure that the
    following instruction is sure to have the correct starting
    address.  Note that while the current location may be changed
    by this directive, nothing is written into the memory locations
    that are skipped over.

    Syntax:

        .ALIGN  <expression>

    Parameter:

        <expression>     An integer expression, indicating the
                         multiple that the current location must
                         match.  Typically, values of 2, 4, or 8
                         are most often used for word, longword,
                         and quadword boundaries.

$ASM ,PSEU,REGI

    The REGION pseudo opcode switches between S0 and P0 address 
    spaces.  The assembler maintains two "current locations",
    one for each of the code regions.  The .BASE directive can
    be used to set the current location for the current region.
    Using the REGION directive selects which current locations
    is to be used.

    After initialization, the P0 base starts at address 00000200
    and the S0 region starts at the first page following the SCB
    and page tables.

    Syntax:

        .REGION  <region>

    Parameter:

        <region>    The region to assemble instructions into. 
                    This can be P0, 0, or PROCESS for the P0
                    region, or S0, 2, or SYSTEM for the S0
                    region.

$ASM ,PSEU,PRIN

    The PRINT opcode is used to display messages during assembly of
    a source file, such as version or copyright information.  This is
    equivalent to the PRINT command and has the same parameters.

    Syntax:

        .PRINT    <expression>  [, <expression> ]

$ASM ,PSEU,BASE

    The BASE opcode is used to set the address (physical or virtual,
    depending on the MAPEN register setting) to begin storing the 
    next instructions or data.  This is equivalent to the console
    command SET BASE.

    Syntax:

        .BASE    <expression>

    Parameter:

        <expression>     Any valid integer expression.  This defines
                         the location where the next instruction or
                         data will be written.

$ASM ,PSEU,BYTE
$ASM ,PSEU,WORD
$ASM ,PSEU,LONG
$ASM ,PSEU,QUAD
$ASM ,PSEU,OCTA

    The BYTE, WORD, LONG, QUAD, and OCTA pseudo-opcodes store specific
    given values of the indicated size in memory: 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16
    bytes.

    Syntax:

        .BYTE    <data> [, <data> ]

        .WORD    <data> [, <data> ]

        .LONG    <data> [, <data> ]

        .QUAD    <data> [, <data> ]

        .OCTA    <data> [, <data> ]


     Parameter:

        <data>     The data value(s) to be stored.  If more than one
                   is given, they must be separated by commas.  The
                   data is stored in memory in the order given.  A
                   BYTE or WORD value may be given signed (-1) or
                   unsigned (255).  A QUAD or OCTA value that is a
                   single number, such as ^X0123456789ABCDEF, is
                   stored in full.  Any other expression is computed
                   as a longword and sign-extended, as eVAX did; in
                   MACRO-32 source it is zero-extended, and a negative
                   number such as -1 is an error, as with VAX MACRO.

$ASM ,PSEU,F_FL
$ASM ,PSEU,D_FL
$ASM ,PSEU,G_FL
$ASM ,PSEU,H_FL

    The F_FLOAT, D_FLOAT, G_FLOATING, and H_FLOATING pseudo-opcodes
    (also F_FLOATING, D_FLOATING, FLOAT, and DOUBLE) store floating
    values in the VAX's four formats: 4, 8, 8, or 16 bytes.

    Syntax:

        .F_FLOAT     <value> [, <value> ]

        .D_FLOAT     <value> [, <value> ]

        .G_FLOATING  <value> [, <value> ]

        .H_FLOATING  <value> [, <value> ]

     Parameter:

        <value>    A decimal number, such as 1.5, -0.1, or 6.02E23.
                   Each is rounded once, from its decimal value, to
                   the format's precision (half away from zero, as
                   the VAX rounds).  A value too large for the format
                   is an error; one too small is stored as zero.

$ASM ,PSEU,PACK

    The PACKED pseudo-opcode stores a packed decimal string: the
    digits two to a byte, most significant first, and the sign in the
    last byte's low four bits (hex C for plus, D for minus).  An even
    number of digits gets a zero first half-byte.  The decimal string
    instructions (MOVP, ADDP4, CVTPL, EDITPC, ...) work on these.

    Syntax:

        .PACKED  <decimal-string> [, <symbol> ]

     Parameter:

        <decimal-string>  A decimal number of 0 to 31 digits, with an
                          optional sign: -12, +500, 0.

        <symbol>          If given, it is set to the number of digits
                          (not counting the sign), the length the
                          decimal instructions take.

$ASM ,PSEU,ENTR

    The ENTRY opcode defines a procedure entry point.  It can be followed
    by an optional register mask specification.  The symbol is stored as
    an entry mask label, and a mask word is generated.

    Syntax:

        .ENTRY    <name> [, <mask> ]

    Parameter:

        <name>    The required name of the entry point.  This may be
                  used as a label in a CALLS or CALLG instruction, or
                  as the entry for a CALL console command.

        <mask>    If specified, this determines the register mask to
                  associate with the procedure.  If ommitted, then a
                  mask of zero (no saved registers) is assumed.  The
                  mask is usually specified as ^m<r2,r3> syntax.

$ASM ,PSEU,END 

    The END opcode terminates the assembly.  If assembling from the
    console terminal, it returns the prompt to the normal VAX> prompt.

    Syntax:

        .END    [<entry>]

    Parameter:

        <entry>        If specified, it indicates the starting entry
                       point for execution.  The symbol __ENTRY is set
                       to this value (which can be any valid address
                       expression).  A CALL instruction is generated to
                       this address to start the program.

                       If you do not want to auto-execute an assembled
                       program, do not include an <entry> value on the
                       END statement.

$ASM ,PSEU,BLKB
$ASM ,PSEU,BLKW
$ASM ,PSEU,BLKL

    The block storage opcodes BLKB, BLKL, and BLKW create a block of
    zeros of byte, longword, or word length.

    Syntax:

        .BLKB      <count>        Creates <count> bytes of zeros.

        .BLKW      <count>        Creates <count> 16-bit words of zeros.

        .BLKL      <count>        Creates <count> 32-bit longwords of 
                                  zeros.

    Parameter:

        <count>        The number of elements to write to the storage.
                       The actual number of bytes is <count> * <size>
                       where <size> is specified by the opcode (byte,
                       word, or longword).

$ASM ,PSEU,ASCI

    The character string storage pseudo-opcodes ASCIC, ASCID, ASCII, and
    ASCIZ all store a string in memory.  The differences are in the format
    of the string storage.

    Syntax:

        .ASCIC     "<string>"      Stores a counted ASCII string.  The
                                   storage contains a byte that holds
                                   the length of <string> (at most 255),
                                   followed by the bytes of the string.

        .ASCID     "<string>"      Stores a string descriptor.  The first
                                   eight bytes are a standard VMS string
                                   descriptor, followed by the actual bytes
                                   of the string.  The descriptor is
                                   initialized with the length and address
                                   of the string data.

        .ASCII     "<string>"      The bytes of the string data are stored
                                   in memory.

        .ASCIZ     "<string>"      A null terminated string is stored.  The
                                   bytes of <string> are written, followed
                                   by an additional byte containing a zero.

    As in VAX MACRO, the string can be delimited by any printing
    character except blank, "=", ";", or "<", as long as it does not
    appear in the string, and lowercase letters are kept.  Between or
    after delimited strings, an expression in angle brackets stores one
    byte, for characters such as a line feed:

        CR = ^D13
        LF = ^D10
        .ASCII    /Line one/<CR><LF>
        .ASCIZ    "It's done."<LF>

    A backslash is an ordinary character; there are no escapes like \n.
    The descriptor .ASCID stores has class S and type T (information
    word 010E).

$ASM ,PSEU,IF  
$ASM ,PSEU,IIF 
$ASM ,PSEU,ENDC
$ASM ,PSEU,IFF 
$ASM ,PSEU,IFT 
$ASM ,PSEU,IFTF
$ASM ,PSEU,IF_F
$ASM ,PSEU,IF_T

    Conditional assembly, as in VAX MACRO.  The statements between .IF
    and its .ENDC are assembled only if the condition is met.  Blocks
    can be nested up to 31 deep.  Statements left out, including any
    labels on them, are not assembled at all.

    Syntax:

        .IF      <condition> <argument>
        ...
        .ENDC

        .IIF     <condition> <argument>, <statement>

    Within an .IF block, these switch the rest of the block on or off:

        .IF_FALSE  (.IFF)       Assemble if the condition was not met
        .IF_TRUE   (.IFT)       Assemble if the condition was met
        .IF_TRUE_FALSE (.IFTF)  Assemble either way

    Conditions (long and short forms), which may be followed by a comma:

        EQUAL  EQ     NOT_EQUAL      NE     <expression> is 0 / not 0
        GREATER  GT   LESS_EQUAL     LE     <expression> is > 0 / <= 0
        LESS_THAN LT  GREATER_EQUAL  GE     <expression> is < 0 / >= 0
        DEFINED  DF   NOT_DEFINED    NDF    <symbol> is defined / not;
                                            A&B needs both, A!B either
        BLANK  B      NOT_BLANK      NB     <text> is blank / not
        IDENTICAL IDN DIFFERENT      DIF    <text>,<text> are the same /
                                            differ

    An expression argument may not use a symbol defined later in the
    program.

    Example:

        .IIF     NOT_DEFINED SS$_NORMAL, .INCLUDE "ssdef.asm"

        .IF      DF DEBUG
        MOVL     #1, R0
        .IF_FALSE
        CLRL     R0
        .ENDC

$ASM ,PSEU,MACR
$ASM ,PSEU,ENDM
$ASM ,PSEU,MDEL
$ASM ,PSEU,MEXI
$ASM ,PSEU,NARG
$ASM ,PSEU,NCHR
$ASM ,PSEU,NTYP

    Macros, as in VAX MACRO.  A macro definition is a named block of
    source; a statement naming the macro (a macro call) assembles the
    block with the call's arguments put in place of the formal ones.
    Macros are tried before instructions, so a macro can replace one.

    Syntax:

        .MACRO   <name> [<formal>[=<default>],...]
        ...
        .ENDM    [<name>]

        <name>   [<argument>,...]

    A formal argument named ?<name> gets a created local label (30000$,
    30001$, ...) when the call gives no argument for it.  A call's
    arguments are positional, or <formal>=<value> in any order; <...>
    or ^x...x encloses one holding commas or blanks, and \<symbol>
    passes a symbol's value in decimal.  An argument is put in place
    everywhere in the block, strings and comments included; an
    apostrophe next to a formal argument's name joins it to the text
    around it (PREFIX'_'N).  Arguments keep their case.

        .MEXIT                  Ends the macro's expansion early
        .MDELETE <name>,...     Deletes macro definitions
        .NARG    <symbol>       The number of positional arguments given
        .NCHR    <symbol>,<text>  The number of characters in <text>
        .NTYPE   <symbol>,<operand>  The addressing mode of <operand>

    In a macro's lines, %LENGTH(<text>), %LOCATE(<text1>,<text2>[,<start>]),
    and %EXTRACT(<start>,<length>,<text>) are replaced by their results.

    The MACRO command also finds macros in macro libraries (see HELP
    MACRO); ASM uses only the macros the source defines.

    Example:

        .MACRO   MAX A, B, DEST, ?SKIP
        MOVL     A, DEST
        CMPL     B, DEST
        BLEQ     SKIP
        MOVL     B, DEST
SKIP:
        .ENDM    MAX

        MAX      R1, R2, R0

$ASM ,PSEU,REPE
$ASM ,PSEU,REPT
$ASM ,PSEU,IRP 
$ASM ,PSEU,IRPC
$ASM ,PSEU,ENDR

    Repeat blocks, as in VAX MACRO: the statements up to .ENDR are
    assembled several times.

    Syntax:

        .REPEAT  <count>        (or .REPT)  <count> times
        .IRP     <symbol>,<<argument>,...>  once for each argument, with
                                            <symbol> replaced by it
        .IRPC    <symbol>,<<text>>          once for each character
        ...
        .ENDR

    .MEXIT in a repeat block ends it, skipping the repetitions left.

    Example:

        .IRP     REG, <R2, R3, R4>
        CLRL     REG
        .ENDR

$ASM ,PSEU,ERRO
$ASM ,PSEU,WARN

    .ERROR and .WARN report a message of the program's own: .ERROR as an
    assembly error, and .WARN as a warning.  The message is the value of
    the expression, if one is given and it isn't zero, then the
    statement's comment.  They're most useful in a macro, in a
    conditional block, to check its arguments.

    Syntax:

        .ERROR   [<expression>] ;<comment>
        .WARN    [<expression>] ;<comment>

    Example:

        .IIF     BLANK <ARG>, .ERROR ; ARG is required

    In the MACRO command, .PRINT displays its comment the same way; the
    ASM command's .PRINT is its own (see HELP ASM PSEUDO PRINT).

$ASM ,PSEU,PTE 

    The .PTE pseudo operator creates a page table entry longword at the current
    location in memory.

$ASM ,PSEU,CASE

    The .CASE pseudo operator is used to construct the branch table that
    follows a CASEB, CASEW, or CASEL instruction.  The branch table must
    be stored in memory immediately following the CASEx instruction.  Each
    element of the branch table is a displacement address of where the
    CASEx will transfer to based on the selector operand.

    This is difficult to code without the .CASE since the table must be
    a set of word displacements from the start of the table.  With the
    .CASE pseudo operator you can simply express it as a list of
    labeled destinations that can be forward references if needed.

    Syntax:

        CASEx selector.rx, base.rx, limit.rx

        .CASE    dest1 [, dest2...]

    Parameters:

        The above syntax specifies that a CASEx opcode must precede the
        .CASE table for it to be meaningful.  This can be followed by one
        or more .CASE pseudo opcodes.  If more than one item is to be
        given for each .CASE then they must be separated by commas.

        Note that an error occurs if the displacement between the label
        and the address of the first .CASE entry cannot be expressed as
        a signed 16-bit word.

    Example:

                  CASEB     (R0), @#BASE_VALUE, #2
                  .CASE     RED
                  .CASE     BLUE
                  .CASE     GREEN
                  MOVL      #ERR_CODE, R0
                  ...
 
        RED:      ADDL2   R2, R3
                  ... 


        In this example, there is a case table of three items.  Note
        that the limit value is zero-based, so for a table of three
        items the range is 0-2 and the limit is 2.  Each .CASE specifies
        the branch destinations in order.  One of these will be chosen
        by the VAX based on the selector in R0, less the base value.
        Note that if the selector value is outside the range allowed
        by the base value and limit, then control resumes after the
        .CASE block with the MOVL of the error code.

        It is an undetected programmer error to specify a .CASE table
        that is not _exactly_ as long as the limit-1 specifies!
